      "latency_ms": 2243.5,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 638,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and one of the most abundant and essential substances on Earth. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, looking at it through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\nChemically, water is represented by the formula **$H_2O$**. This means every single molecule of water is made up of:\n*   Two atoms of **Hydrogen** (H)\n*   One atom of **Oxygen** (O)\n\nThese atoms are held together by covalent bonds. Because oxygen attracts electrons more strongly than hydrogen, water is a **polar molecule**. This means the oxygen side has a slight negative charge, and the hydrogen side has a slight positive charge. This polarity is what makes water such a great solvent—it can dissolve many substances, like salt and sugar.\n\n### 2. The Physical View\nWater is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three distinct states of matter:\n*   **Liquid:** The fluid we drink, swim in, and rain from the sky.\n*   **Solid (Ice):** Frozen water. Uniquely, solid water (ice) is *less dense* than liquid water, which is why ice floats. If ice sank, oceans would freeze from the bottom up, destroying aquatic life.\n*   **Gas (Vapor/Steam):** Invisible water vapor in the air or steam rising from a boiling pot.\n\nWater also has a very high **heat capacity**, meaning it can absorb and release large amounts of heat without its temperature changing drastically. This helps regulate the climate of the Earth and the body temperatures of living organisms.\n\n### 3. The Biological View\nWater is the cradle of life. Life as we know it cannot exist without it:\n*   **Human Body:** The human body is made up of about 60% water. The brain is roughly 73% water, and the lungs are about 83%.\n*   **Cellular Function:** Water acts as the medium in which all the metabolic and cellular processes of life take place. It transports nutrients, flushes out waste, and regulates body temperature.\n\n### 4. The Ecological and Geographical View\nWater covers about **71% of the Earth's surface**, mostly in oceans and seas (about 96.5% of Earth's water is saltwater). \n*   Only about **2.5% is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. \n*   Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible freshwater in lakes, rivers, and ponds for human use.\n*   Water is constantly moving through the **water cycle** (evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and collection), recycling itself continuously across the planet.\n\n### Summary\nIn short, water is a simple molecule that performs miraculous jobs. It is the lifeblood of ecosystems, the regulator of global climate, the chemical solvent of life, and the resource that connects all living things on Earth."
